% ============================================================================
% muscle-nuclei-count — for skeletal and cardiac muscle, whether the muscle
% fiber is multinucleated or has a single nucleus, as the SAME SEER spans
% already quoted in tissue-types.adj also state (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `tissue-types.adj`
% (`tissue_example(tissue, example)`, a representative example/location for
% each of the four basic tissue types — muscle → cardiac_or_skeletal). That
% table's own header already quotes, verbatim, two NCI SEER sentences
% describing skeletal and cardiac muscle fibers in more detail than the
% tissue-example schema captures, and buried in those spans, alongside the
% striation fact already decoded by the sibling `muscle-striation.adj` (off
% the DIFFERENT parent table `muscle-types.adj`, which cites the SAME SEER
% muscle page), is a second, independent leftover fact: how many nuclei
% each fiber has.
%
%   skeletal → multinucleated
%     "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, MULTINUCLEATED, striated,
%      and under voluntary control."
%
%   cardiac → single_nucleus
%     "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, ONE NUCLEUS PER CELL,
%      striations, and intercalated disks."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? muscle_nuclei_count(skeletal, $Nuclei)   % multinucleated
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, an ordinary two-column EXACT
% lookup): each `row` lowers to a ground relation `muscle_nuclei_count(
% muscle, nuclei)` carrying THAT ROW'S OWN citation, with the envelope's
% locator and trust inherited (RS-5e, #14986), so the lookup above is the
% ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the nuclei-count fact
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls. Only skeletal and
% cardiac muscle have a nuclei-count fact stated in this cited span
% (smooth muscle, tabled by the sibling `muscle-striation.adj`, is not named
% in the two sentences `tissue-types.adj` quotes in its header -- it IS named
% in that table's shipped `cites`, which states no nuclei count), so this
% sibling is HONESTLY NARROW: it grounds exactly these two muscle types and
% abstains on smooth muscle or any other word. The query also runs BACKWARD --
% bind a nuclei value and recall which muscle type has it:
%
%     ? muscle_nuclei_count($Muscle, single_nucleus)
%       % cardiac
%
% Provenance: EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN span as its `source` (RS-5e,
% #14986); the locator and trust are inherited from the envelope
% field-by-field. Until this change the envelope WAS the skeletal
% sentence and the cardiac sentence rode as a table-level `cites`, so a
% `cardiac` recall was warranted primarily by a sentence about SKELETAL
% muscle and its own sentence reached no answer. Relocating it empties
% the corroborations array on every answer.
%
% The table-level `source` is now the page's framing sentence, "Muscle
% tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to shorten or
% contract in order to produce movement of the body parts.", which names
% NO muscle type -- not skeletal, not cardiac, and not SMOOTH, which is a
% real third type this page describes and which is deliberately no row
% here. Naming no ROW KEY is not enough for a frame: a sentence about
% smooth muscle names neither row and would still frame the wrong thing.
% A test arm forbids the whole taxonomy.
%
% ONE NARROWING, because this paragraph talks about the PAGE while the
% paragraph above talks about a narrower scope. "Only skeletal and cardiac
% muscle have a nuclei-count fact stated in this cited span" is true of the
% TWO SENTENCES `tissue-types.adj` QUOTES IN ITS HEADER, which is where this
% table's two facts were mined from. It is not true of that table's shipped
% `cites` either -- that span reads "Muscle tissue can be categorized into
% skeletal muscle tissue, smooth muscle tissue, and cardiac muscle tissue."
% (final period included, because a quotation missing one is the error class
% this batch exists to remove), which names smooth and gives no nuclei count
% at all. And it is not true of
% the page, which states one for smooth muscle too ("Smooth muscle cells are
% spindle shaped, have a single, centrally located nucleus, and lack
% striations."). Smooth muscle stays out of this table for the reason given
% above, not for want of a stated fact.
%
% Both spans and the envelope were re-measured on the cited page: each
% occurs exactly once, and zero times against a 404 control on the same
% host. The page carries no U+00A0, U+2013 or U+2019 -- in fact no non-ASCII
% codepoints at all -- so there is no invisible-byte hazard here, checked
% rather than assumed. Shards 03570 (`mixture-types`, an NBSP envelope) and
% 03590 (`sun-layer`, an en dash and a right single quote) each shipped one;
% 03580 (`blood-cell-types`) did not, so this is not a property of every
% conversion in the batch.
%
% This library carries the SAME two NCI SEER Training Modules sentences already
% quoted inside `tissue-types.adj`'s own header (its
% `muscle → cardiac_or_skeletal` row), decoding with a different schema the
% nuclei-count half of each. The paragraph above used to end "no new WebFetch";
% that is retired, because the spans ARE now measured against the page itself
% (see the re-measurement above) -- the same retirement the sibling
% `muscle-striation.adj` already made.
%
% This is a DIFFERENT leftover fact from the one `muscle-striation.adj`
% already decoded off `muscle-types.adj` (a different existing parent
% table that happens to cite the same underlying SEER muscle page) — not a
% duplicate, since each parent table is its own distinct library with its
% own citation envelope, and "striated" and "nuclei count" are two
% independent facts sitting in the same source sentences. `trust
% authoritative` — the same tier `tissue-types.adj` already carries (U.S.
% National Cancer Institute, a primary U.S. government teaching resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table muscle_nuclei_count {
    columns muscle, nuclei

    row (skeletal, multinucleated) {
        source "Skeletal muscle fibers are cylindrical, multinucleated, striated, and under voluntary control."
    }
    row (cardiac, single_nucleus) {
        source "Cardiac muscle has branching fibers, one nucleus per cell, striations, and intercalated disks."
    }

    source "Muscle tissue is composed of cells that have the special ability to shorten or contract in order to produce movement of the body parts."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cells_tissues_membranes/tissues/muscle.html"
    trust authoritative
}
